    {
      "id": "DC_kwDOKrJqmc4A74s0",
      "body": "Any objections to me starting with adding TLS support then?",
      "author": {
        "login": "BeigeBox",
        "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1066823?u=6b667f87093c42638442e799d50e0cc2373dfacc&v=4",
        "url": "https://github.com/BeigeBox"
      },
      "created_at": "2026-02-04T21:48:10Z",
      "updated_at": "2026-02-04T21:48:10Z",
      "is_answer": false,
      "replies": [
        {
          "id": "DC_kwDOKrJqmc4A76w6",
          "body": "@BeigeBox\r\n\r\nI am concerned that with the self-signed cert it will just condition users to ignore those warnings without adding much security.\r\n\r\nLike mentioned in the other issue, right now our security boundary is the WiFi network itself, and so in order to revoke access you just gotta change the wifi password.\r\n\r\n**I don't agree that a password is useless without TLS**. A device having access to the WiFi network does not automatically mean it's able to observe all packets within that network. A device may run malicious software that doesn't have the right permissions to capture packets, but still is able to send network requests.\r\n\r\nBut: Most of the devices we install rayhunter on have glaring security holes, and letting a malicious device onto its WiFi network is going to be problem in other ways. We use those security holes to install rayhunter.\r\n\r\nSo I think it'll not fix much in this situation, and I don't think it's worth the effort.\r\n\r\nInstead we should probably figure out if we can disable the WiFi AP in a cross-platform way, which would fix a ton of issues at once (detectability of rayhunter devices too). It seems people already do that and connect via USB tethering only.",
          "author": {
            "login": "untitaker",
            "avatar_url": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/837573?u=4b106f1ba83b3028677a861efe31c46d703e77ff&v=4",
            "url": "https://github.com/untitaker"
